1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
It's found on everything from spoons to skyscrapers, in kitchens and on cars.

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:11,000
It just glitters no matter what you do.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Nearly 40 million tons are dug up from the earth each year,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
putting sparkle and shine into a 15 billion dollar industry.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Girls like diamonds, guys like chrome.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
You'll never look at a motorcycle the same way again.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,000
This is Addicted to Chrome on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,220
Chrome. Even the word sounds lustrous.

9
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:52,360
For some, it's a subtle detail.

10
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:54,640
For others, a bold statement.

11
00:00:54,641 --> 00:00:59,220
But for hot rod enthusiast Mike Herra, chrome is pretty much everything.

12
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:07,880
I don't think you can have enough chrome on a car.

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,340
I think the chrome is the whole heart of the car.

14
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,740
This is a 1943 Packard Dash 5 2500 Flyer.

15
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,100
Probably 80% of the car is chrome.

16
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:22,760
The very front is my favorite part of the car.

17
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,480
It's out of a tractor trailer.

18
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:26,700
It's a drop front end.

19
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:27,900
It's all chrome plated.

20
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,600
The calipers are chrome plated.

21
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:31,360
The disc brakes are plated.

22
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:34,020
The front end, the drag links, the headlights.

23
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:36,180
It's probably the most dramatic part of the car.

24
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:44,600
Only the 30-foot-long body of the 16,000-pound metal Roadster lacks the true luster of chrome.

25
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,720
The car itself is all aluminum, and then we polished it out to make it look like chrome

26
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:53,480
because chrome has to be put into tanks,

27
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:55,160
and then it's electroplated,

28
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,440
so they don't make tanks large enough to put the whole car in.

29
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,620
If we could get it in the tanks, we would have plated it.

30
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,220
Amazingly, there's not really much chrome on the car.

31
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:08,340
The chrome plating on the parts is just millionths of an inch thick.

32
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:10,500
But more on that later.

33
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:14,400
Perhaps the most dazzling part of the Chrome Mobile

34
00:02:14,401 --> 00:02:16,300
is its fire-breathing engine.

35
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,360
The engine was originally out of a 1943 PT boat,

36
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,820
and we converted it and built this entire car around it.

37
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:29,880
And it's the ultimate chrome machine.

38
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:34,720
It puts out 3,000 horsepower, and it's 2,500 cubic inches.

39
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:36,500
The fastest we've had it was about 125.

40
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:39,200
Still had a lot of power. It was still climbing.

41
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:41,180
We were just not sure we could stop it.

42
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:46,320
Mike spent more than $1 million

43
00:02:46,321 --> 00:02:48,720
custom-building the gleaming chrome beast.

44
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:54,971
I think it's everything. I think it adds luster, you know, a mystery to the car.

45
00:02:54,972 --> 00:02:56,140
It just makes the car.

46
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:02,720
So just what is this mysterious gleaming substance we call chrome?

47
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:09,100
Chrome is short for chromium, which is highly reflective metal resistant to tarnish and corrosion.

48
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:16,680
That's because upon contact with the atmosphere, the metal develops a protective oxide coating.

49
00:03:16,681 --> 00:03:23,560
It's just like iron rust, but clear.

50
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:31,460
Perhaps nobody depends on chrome's corrosion resistance and bright sheen more than truckers.

51
00:03:31,461 --> 00:03:33,520
Everybody wants to have a cool looking truck.

52
00:03:33,521 --> 00:03:37,140
We have a lot of time to think driving down the road of stuff to add and do to your truck,

53
00:03:37,141 --> 00:03:38,740
so you know, it's endless.

54
00:03:38,741 --> 00:03:42,480
If we didn't have chrome, you know, all the truck drivers would be lost.

55
00:03:42,481 --> 00:03:46,280
We wouldn't have nothing to cover up all this stuff.

56
00:03:46,281 --> 00:03:53,080
At Poor State Trucks in Joplin, Missouri, tens of thousands of truckers make their way to this treasure trove of chrome.

57
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:59,280
It's the home of the self-proclaimed Chrome Shop Mafia.

58
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:13,840
Truckers go 40, 50, sometimes 100 miles out of route to come by here, visit the store, and get their chrome fix.

59
00:04:13,841 --> 00:04:17,740
Whether it's a little gadget they want for their dash, you know, a nice shiny front bumper,

60
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:21,280
even these big exhaust pipes we sell in stock, they've got to have their chrome.

61
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:22,980
They're addicted to chrome sometimes.

62
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:26,800
For truckers looking to add some serious bling to their big rigs,

63
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:29,400
the chrome shop Moppy knows all the tricks.

64
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:39,400
At their on-site installation room, this Peterbilt 379 is in the middle of an extreme chrome makeover.

65
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,540
We're almost done chroming it out.

66
00:04:43,541 --> 00:04:48,540
We have a few finishing touches to put on it, such as a chrome-plated .22 to a .20 tapered bumper,

67
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:52,240
an exhaust stack, some chrome-plated plastic hub covers,

68
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,300
a chrome spot mirror on the mirrors up here by the door,

69
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:57,900
and just various little knick-knacky stuff inside.

70
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:05,240
Swapping out the bumper and adding minor chrome accents takes a little more than a few nuts and bolts.

71
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:12,960
But attaching the 9-foot-tall, 100-pound chrome exhaust stack

72
00:05:12,961 --> 00:05:15,300
requires some real elbow grease.

73
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:17,160
We made our own T-pipe.

74
00:05:17,161 --> 00:05:18,840
It's 10 inches as well as the pipe is,

75
00:05:18,841 --> 00:05:21,460
and we're trying to slide this pipe into our T-pipe.

76
00:05:21,461 --> 00:05:23,140
It's not the easiest thing in the world to maneuver

77
00:05:23,141 --> 00:05:25,340
because it's so big and awkward and heavy,

78
00:05:25,341 --> 00:05:26,880
but it fills up this entire gap,

79
00:05:26,881 --> 00:05:28,800
so you don't have any open cavities on your truck.

80
00:05:28,801 --> 00:05:30,680
So it's well worth it in the end.

81
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:38,820
Of course, there's always a cheaper alternative to chrome,

82
00:05:38,821 --> 00:05:41,460
but that means sacrificing the unique glint of the metal.

83
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:47,120
On these big rigs, there's a lot of chrome on it, but there's also a lot of stainless steel.

84
00:05:47,121 --> 00:05:50,020
And from a distance, they're all shiny. They're all somewhat reflective.

85
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:54,940
But here's something for comparison. This is just a raw piece of stainless steel.

86
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:58,960
And we can take the yardstick, kind of hold it up there and look into it like a mirror.

87
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:02,660
You can see the image there, but it's a little foggy, a little blurry.

88
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:05,740
Now take a look at the chrome-plated finish down here on this bumper.

89
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:08,518
I mean, look at that.

90
00:06:08,519 --> 00:06:11,420
You can read every number right down to the hash marks, you know?

91
00:06:11,421 --> 00:06:14,000
that's a true mirror image.

92
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:15,480
That's a good thing about chrome plating.

93
00:06:17,220 --> 00:06:19,640
But chrome's origins are not so flashy.

94
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:25,100
The metal can be traced back to late 3rd century B.C. Chinese weapons.

95
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,320
Although buried more than 2,000 years ago,

96
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:31,900
ancient bronze tips of swords

97
00:06:31,901 --> 00:06:34,560
found at the Terracotta Army burial pits

98
00:06:34,561 --> 00:06:36,640
showed no sign of corrosion.

99
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:41,160
Scientists discovered the ancient weapon makers

100
00:06:41,161 --> 00:06:45,900
unknowingly created a bronze that contained 2% chromium.

101
00:06:45,901 --> 00:06:48,500
Probably fires in the mausoleum caused the chromium

102
00:06:48,501 --> 00:06:51,780
to migrate to the surface of the swords.

103
00:06:51,781 --> 00:06:55,400
But it wasn't until 1797 that French chemist

104
00:06:55,401 --> 00:06:59,280
Nicolas-Louis Vaucelin first discovered the lustrous metal

105
00:06:59,281 --> 00:07:02,520
in a red lead ore from Siberia.

106
00:07:02,521 --> 00:07:05,580
He named it chromium, after the Greek word chroma,

107
00:07:05,581 --> 00:07:08,260
meaning color, since many of its compounds

108
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:10,560
produce vibrant colors.

109
00:07:10,561 --> 00:07:15,040
And for more than a century, chromium's primary use was in paints and dyes.

110
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:26,160
It wasn't until the early 1900s that metallurgists discovered chrome's corrosion resistance in the production of steel.

111
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:29,520
Chromium is very important in the manufacturing of stainless steel.

112
00:07:29,521 --> 00:07:34,460
If it wasn't for the addition of chrome, stainless steel would no longer be stain resistant.

113
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:40,380
Today, metal alloys account for 85% of chromium use.

114
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:48,280
Almost 40% of the primary commercial ore, called chromite, is produced in South Africa.

115
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:55,120
The ore is also abundant in India, Russia, Kazakhstan, and Turkey.

116
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:05,240
Today, world production of chromium is nearly 40 million tons a year, a market that's worth about $15 billion.

117
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:09,300
The flashiest use of chrome is plating it onto other metals.

118
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:15,780
In fact, objects are never made of solid chrome, but rather covered in a thin layer of the metal.

119
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:20,200
So why not make a solid chrome bumper?

120
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:27,940
Ultimately, it wouldn't be any shinier or more durable, but would add unnecessary weight and more than double the price.

121
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:30,340
Chrome.

122
00:08:30,341 --> 00:08:35,480
Its sparkle and shine have transformed everything from truck bumpers to silverware.

123
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:38,440
But that beautiful finish doesn't come from nowhere.

124
00:08:38,441 --> 00:08:43,440
The process of chrome plating is no small task.

125
00:08:43,441 --> 00:08:50,740
At the Arlington Plating Company in Palatine, Illinois, technicians chrome plate more than

126
00:08:50,741 --> 00:08:55,740
15,000 aluminum and magnesium parts each week.

127
00:08:55,741 --> 00:09:01,680
To get the chrome to stick to the parts, technicians turn to electroplating.

128
00:09:01,681 --> 00:09:06,700
It's the process of coating an electrically conductive object with a layer of metal using

129
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:33,700
direct current.

130
00:09:33,701 --> 00:09:36,860
The hanging baskets on each positively charged side

131
00:09:36,861 --> 00:09:40,780
contain the metal to be plated to the part.

132
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:45,140
The 2,500 gallon tank is filled with an acidic solution

133
00:09:45,141 --> 00:09:47,860
that permits the flow of electricity.

134
00:09:47,861 --> 00:09:50,560
Since the part itself is negatively charged,

135
00:09:50,561 --> 00:09:53,560
it attracts the positively charged metal from the baskets.

136
00:09:56,300 --> 00:10:00,860
But creating a chrome shiny finish isn't just a quick dip.

137
00:10:00,861 --> 00:10:03,220
In their large scale electroplating line,

138
00:10:03,221 --> 00:10:08,980
truck bumpers and step tubes travel back and forth for more than three hours between 28

139
00:10:08,981 --> 00:10:14,900
tanks. The process begins with a 30-minute pre-treatment of several hot cleaners and

140
00:10:14,901 --> 00:10:20,928
a nitric acid blend to ensure the purest and smoothest aluminum surface.

141
00:10:20,929 --> 00:10:22,100
And before the

142
00:10:22,101 --> 00:10:26,720
bumper can get its flashy coat of chrome, it goes through four tanks of nickel for more

143
00:10:26,721 --> 00:10:27,720
than two hours.

144
00:10:27,721 --> 00:10:34,120
If I was just to put the chrome on there, it would not look shiny and beautiful and

145
00:10:34,121 --> 00:10:36,720
bright like everybody thinks chrome is.

146
00:10:36,721 --> 00:10:39,720
And when you pour it out after the nickel tank, it looks yellow.

147
00:10:39,721 --> 00:10:43,720
And if you put it out on a vehicle, it'll tarnish very quickly.

148
00:10:43,721 --> 00:10:46,720
The chrome gives it that nice blue depth to it.

149
00:10:46,721 --> 00:10:51,720
And it allows, obviously, for it to last quite some time out in the field.

150
00:10:51,721 --> 00:10:56,720
While the aluminum bumper is just over an eighth of an inch thick, the nickel layer

151
00:10:56,721 --> 00:10:59,520
is only 15 ten-thousands of an inch thick,

152
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:01,320
and the outer chrome layer

153
00:11:01,321 --> 00:11:04,360
is just 15 millionths of an inch thick.

154
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:09,360
That's more than 250 times thinner

155
00:11:09,361 --> 00:11:10,940
than a human hair.

156
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:14,840
Even so, it adds a sparkle

157
00:11:14,841 --> 00:11:17,320
and the utmost resistance to corrosion.

158
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:20,720
To test this resistance,

159
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:23,020
Arlington Plating Company technicians

160
00:11:23,021 --> 00:11:24,920
turn to a salt spray chamber,

161
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:28,060
Placing parts in the chamber for just eight hours

162
00:11:28,061 --> 00:11:31,100
simulates more than two years of real-life use.

163
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:37,040
This is an unplated part, just raw steel part.

164
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:40,200
As you can see, it rusted, the iron rusted, forming an iron oxide.

165
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:43,140
This is a nickel-plated part, slight rusting, a little bit of yellow.

166
00:11:43,141 --> 00:11:44,720
And then we have the chrome-plated parts.

167
00:11:45,060 --> 00:11:49,920
As you can see, virtually flawless finish, no visible rusting at all.

168
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:53,720
Chrome actually does rust like all other metals,

169
00:11:53,721 --> 00:11:55,480
but at a much slower rate.

170
00:11:56,140 --> 00:11:58,140
And unlike the eyesore of iron oxide,

171
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:00,120
chrome oxide is clear.

172
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:05,440
Back at the chrome plating line,

173
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:07,940
the part finally makes its way to the chrome tank,

174
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:10,420
which requires a unique electroplating technique.

175
00:12:12,260 --> 00:12:13,960
We're here at a final step,

176
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:16,140
which is the actual chrome process itself.

177
00:12:16,141 --> 00:12:18,620
The chrome here is actually in the solution.

178
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:20,260
There are no chrome anodes.

179
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:22,600
We use lead anodes to give us the conductivity

180
00:12:22,601 --> 00:12:25,660
and the throw we need to place the chrome on the part.

181
00:12:26,600 --> 00:12:29,180
Finally, after just one minute in the chrome tank

182
00:12:29,181 --> 00:12:32,700
and then a few dunks in water to rinse off the golden chrome solution,

183
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:35,380
this truck bumper is ready to hit the road.

184
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:40,100
But not all chrome plating is so shiny and decorative.

185
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:47,420
Hard chrome plating is applied directly to an object

186
00:12:47,421 --> 00:12:50,560
to create a thicker, more protective outer layer.

187
00:12:50,561 --> 00:12:57,560
While the chrome is still metallic, it's not very reflective, since there's no nickel substrate.

188
00:12:57,561 --> 00:13:04,560
The hard chrome is used more in functional coating for wear, for lubricity, for hardness.

189
00:13:04,561 --> 00:13:13,560
Now, a part in hard chrome could be in a chrome tank actually for as long as hours.

190
00:13:13,561 --> 00:13:18,480
At Arlington Plating, motorcycle pistons spend 10 minutes in the chrome tank,

191
00:13:19,260 --> 00:13:24,640
creating a layer of metal that is 500 times thicker than the chrome on decorative parts.

192
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:29,420
Of course, most bikers aren't satisfied with just the duller hard chrome parts.

193
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:32,440
They want the shiny stuff, too.

194
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:41,740
At Orange County Harley-Davidson in Southern California,

195
00:13:41,741 --> 00:13:48,740
Bikers gather for a ride almost every weekend to show off their flashy choppers.

196
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:56,740
I've always said to my friends is that if there's no chrome in heaven, I'm not going.

197
00:13:56,741 --> 00:14:02,740
I love to chrome things and I love to customize it and make my bike unique to me.

198
00:14:02,741 --> 00:14:07,740
This is an ultra classic, full dresser, bagger they call them.

199
00:14:07,741 --> 00:14:08,687
they call them.

200
00:14:08,688 --> 00:14:13,420
I put chrome legs, I put chrome wheels, I change the calipers, I change the

201
00:14:13,421 --> 00:14:18,620
brake discs, all chrome. So when it's rolling, it's a shining.

202
00:14:18,621 --> 00:14:21,220
It just glitters no matter what

203
00:14:21,221 --> 00:14:25,640
you do. It's just like a ring. Girls like diamonds, guys like chrome.

204
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:32,900
For more than a century, chromium has captivated us as one of the most luminous metals found on

205
00:14:32,901 --> 00:14:43,420
Sure, it's all over big rigs and motorcycles, but when it comes to chrome flash, nothing beats the shiny display of eye candy at a classic car show.

206
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:48,677
This is a 1953 Cadillac Coupe de Ville. It has quite a large amount of chrome on it.

207
00:14:48,678 --> 00:14:52,420
It's just a wonderful car to own. It attracts a lot of attention.

208
00:14:52,421 --> 00:15:04,620
In Santa Ana, California, more than 200 car enthusiasts gather each month to pay homage to the golden, or should we say silver age, of chrome.

209
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,660
It actually started in 1926, was the first car.

210
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:18,700
It was a 1926 Oldsmobile, and it became a marketing tool in the designer's features because originally they had brass, they had copper.

211
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:22,220
Well, all of that would tarnish and would tarnish very quickly.

212
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:30,700
Soon, automakers used the metal to adorn everything, from the front bumper to the taillights.

213
00:15:30,701 --> 00:15:37,040
The superior corrosion resistance and unmatched polish of chrome caught on with consumers.

214
00:15:37,041 --> 00:15:43,400
By the 1950s, chrome virtually unknown 30 years earlier had become a household word

215
00:15:43,401 --> 00:15:47,560
and a standard in car design.

216
00:15:47,561 --> 00:15:51,380
Case in point, this 1954 Packard Caribbean.

217
00:15:51,381 --> 00:15:56,440
At the time they designed this car, what they were looking for is sales, and the chrome

218
00:15:56,441 --> 00:15:59,040
basically sold the car.

219
00:15:59,041 --> 00:16:03,640
If you removed all the chrome and painted all this, it would actually have not probably

220
00:16:03,641 --> 00:16:09,240
resulted in a lot of sales.

221
00:16:09,241 --> 00:16:16,600
The use of this aptly named bright work peaked in 1958 with the Oldsmobile 98 sedan, believed

222
00:16:16,601 --> 00:16:22,760
to have more chrome per square inch than any other car from the decade.

223
00:16:22,761 --> 00:16:28,480
While grinning wide-mouthed grills and soaring tail fins may dazzle with their bold sparkle,

224
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:33,900
perhaps the most intricate and unique chrome piece on a classic car is the hood ornament,

225
00:16:33,901 --> 00:16:38,800
or mascot, as collectors call them.

226
00:16:38,801 --> 00:16:45,000
And nobody loved mascots like classic car collector and restoration expert Don Sommer,

227
00:16:45,001 --> 00:16:48,660
who has gathered and preserved more than 3,000 sparkling chrome pieces.

228
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:50,920
Here's a pretty one.

229
00:16:51,240 --> 00:16:54,380
This is a new old stock 3031 Cadillac Heron.

230
00:16:55,680 --> 00:17:00,080
But another really pretty one is this Packard Adonis,

231
00:17:00,760 --> 00:17:02,140
sometimes called a sliding boy.

232
00:17:02,360 --> 00:17:06,000
This particular cap came out in 29, and they used it up through 31

233
00:17:06,001 --> 00:17:10,080
and then limited a little bit from 32 to 36.

234
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:13,680
Some mascots were rigged to do more than just glisten in the sun.

235
00:17:13,681 --> 00:17:16,020
Well, this is the Action Twins.

236
00:17:16,021 --> 00:17:19,920
And there were a number of ornaments that operated with the wind,

237
00:17:19,921 --> 00:17:20,920
and this is one of them.

238
00:17:20,921 --> 00:17:23,120
And so as the car moved forward through the wind,

239
00:17:23,121 --> 00:17:25,520
it turned a propeller, and that made the figures move.

240
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:31,760
Most of the miniature chrome sculptures in the collection

241
00:17:31,761 --> 00:17:34,400
are worth a few hundred dollars apiece.

242
00:17:34,401 --> 00:17:37,680
Others are so unique, they're valued in the thousands.

243
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:43,200
The most rare mascot that I have out here on the table is this boat.

244
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:46,180
This is the only one known in the world.

245
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:49,520
Looks like sort of a takeoff of a PT boat.

246
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:54,000
And another rare one here is this Packard radio antenna,

247
00:17:54,440 --> 00:17:55,980
and that was an actual antenna.

248
00:17:56,360 --> 00:17:58,940
I think I paid $2,500 at the time for it.

249
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:03,340
Mascots weren't always so shiny and ornate.

250
00:18:03,860 --> 00:18:06,280
In fact, they began as functional temperature meters

251
00:18:06,281 --> 00:18:09,080
mounted to the radiator caps on the front of cars.

252
00:18:09,081 --> 00:18:14,440
Here's an example of one that was on a Cleveland car.

253
00:18:14,441 --> 00:18:16,080
And these are called motometers.

254
00:18:16,081 --> 00:18:20,480
And they told you what the temperature was in the radiator.

255
00:18:20,481 --> 00:18:26,240
When automakers moved the temperature gauge to the dash in the late 1920s,

256
00:18:26,241 --> 00:18:30,840
they replaced the motometer with ornaments to cover up the humble radiator cap.

257
00:18:30,841 --> 00:18:37,520
By the 1930s, elaborate chrome-plated hood ornaments were standard.

258
00:18:37,521 --> 00:18:44,200
It wasn't until 1958 that automakers started removing the chrome accents, since they posed

259
00:18:44,201 --> 00:18:51,440
a high risk for injuries in collisions with pedestrians.

260
00:18:51,441 --> 00:18:56,600
Beyond collecting the chrome figurines, Semmer also created and sold replicas of them for

261
00:18:56,601 --> 00:18:59,360
more than 40 years.

262
00:18:59,361 --> 00:19:06,520
Out of the 230 chrome mascots he made, the Pierce Arrow Archer shines above the rest.

263
00:19:06,521 --> 00:19:11,120
I make it in two pieces, the cap and the ornament separate.

264
00:19:11,121 --> 00:19:16,340
When it gets all done, it's about $800 for the combination.

265
00:19:16,341 --> 00:19:21,160
To recreate the chrome archer, Summer utilized the lost wax method.

266
00:19:21,161 --> 00:19:25,180
It started at his shop with a meticulously shaped wax mold.

267
00:19:25,181 --> 00:19:31,140
From there, the mold headed to a foundry, where it was cast in stucco.

268
00:19:31,141 --> 00:19:36,500
It was developed in ancient times for statuary, but it's a way you can make some very

269
00:19:36,501 --> 00:19:42,580
complicated casting. After melting the wax out, workers pour

270
00:19:42,581 --> 00:19:48,320
molten stainless steel into the stucco shell. Once cooled down and knocked from

271
00:19:48,321 --> 00:19:54,380
its shell, the stainless steel mascot is polished and then moves on to a local

272
00:19:54,381 --> 00:19:58,740
chrome plating shop. There, workers flash plate it for just 15 seconds in

273
00:19:58,741 --> 00:20:08,920
chrome to give it that desirable sheen.

274
00:20:08,921 --> 00:20:16,080
The optimistic age of fins and chrome was cut short by the oil crisis of the 1970s.

275
00:20:16,081 --> 00:20:20,840
New emission regulations and fuel economy restrictions forced automakers to change their

276
00:20:20,841 --> 00:20:26,780
designs and the heavy steel with chrome plating was kicked to the curb.

277
00:20:26,781 --> 00:20:27,920
took its place.

278
00:20:29,320 --> 00:20:31,960
But thanks to new electroplating technology,

279
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:35,980
chrome is making a comeback in the form of plastic.

280
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:40,600
Much like cars in the past that used a lot of decorative metal parts,

281
00:20:41,180 --> 00:20:44,760
vehicles today have a lot of plastic components that are chrome-plated on them.

282
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:49,480
Starting out with this vehicle, we have a chrome-plated plastic grille.

283
00:20:49,860 --> 00:20:53,200
Along with the grille, we have emblems or nameplates, as they're called,

284
00:20:53,201 --> 00:20:58,400
the skull cap on the mirror, door handles, as well as the body side moldings.

285
00:20:59,440 --> 00:21:06,160
The 450,000 square foot SRG Global Plastic Chrome Plating Facility in Moorhead, Kentucky

286
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:10,663
chromes as many as 15,000 plastic parts every day.

287
00:21:10,664 --> 00:21:13,840
Behind me is an injection molding machine,

288
00:21:13,841 --> 00:21:18,411
one of 28 we have here in the plant. This particular machine is molding a grill.

289
00:21:18,412 --> 00:21:19,112
The process

290
00:21:19,113 --> 00:21:21,280
The process starts with the bulk material.

291
00:21:21,281 --> 00:21:24,360
The bulk material is fed into the barrel of the machine.

292
00:21:24,361 --> 00:21:28,760
The barrel melts the material from bulk form into a molten form.

293
00:21:28,761 --> 00:21:33,800
When it's in a molten form, it gets shot or injected into the molding tool you see behind

294
00:21:33,801 --> 00:21:34,800
us.

295
00:21:34,801 --> 00:21:43,620
Not only is chrome plating plastic more cost-effective than using a steel substrate,

296
00:21:43,621 --> 00:21:49,000
but it's also lighter and ultimately more fuel efficient.

297
00:21:49,001 --> 00:21:54,240
Before the molded parts can get chrome plated, they must undergo an acid etching process

298
00:21:54,241 --> 00:21:57,240
so that the surface of the plastic behaves like a metal.

299
00:21:57,241 --> 00:22:02,240
We're at the beginning part of the process here, which is called the etching phase.

300
00:22:02,241 --> 00:22:07,560
At this part of the process is where we use a blend of chromic acid, sulfuric acid, along

301
00:22:07,561 --> 00:22:12,140
with heat to create frosty onto the surface of the part.

302
00:22:12,141 --> 00:22:17,460
The acid solution eats away at the plastic for about 10 minutes, creating microscopic

303
00:22:17,461 --> 00:22:20,500
craters on the part.

304
00:22:20,501 --> 00:22:26,620
Next, the sponge-like surface absorbs palladium to make up for plastic's inherently low static

305
00:22:26,621 --> 00:22:28,680
electric charge.

306
00:22:28,681 --> 00:22:33,280
The palladium deposits ultimately allow a chemical reaction to occur with nickel.

307
00:22:33,281 --> 00:22:39,060
The bath that we're in right now is called the Electroless Nickel Bath.

308
00:22:39,061 --> 00:22:43,220
In this part of the process, the nickel now begins to react with that palladium and begins

309
00:22:43,221 --> 00:22:47,400
to deposit in those small little crevices and then grows out along the surface of the

310
00:22:47,401 --> 00:22:48,400
part.

311
00:22:48,401 --> 00:22:53,460
And now it begins to encapsulate it at about 10 to 12 millionths of an inch.

312
00:22:53,461 --> 00:22:59,780
Finally, the part is ready for its plunge into the chrome electroplating tank.

313
00:22:59,781 --> 00:23:02,680
The end result?

314
00:23:02,681 --> 00:23:08,260
A lightweight alternative to metal that's just as brilliant.

315
00:23:08,261 --> 00:23:11,220
And it's used on more than 7 million cars each year.

316
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:20,220
Our houses are loaded with chrome.

317
00:23:20,221 --> 00:23:27,220
But chrome-plated fixtures are just a small portion of the chrome in our homes.

318
00:23:27,221 --> 00:23:33,220
The metal's real dominance comes because of its ability to make steel stainless.

319
00:23:33,221 --> 00:23:40,220
In fact, almost 70% of all chrome is used in the production of stainless steel.

320
00:23:43,220 --> 00:23:53,360
At North American Stainless in Ghent, Kentucky, more than 3,000 tons of the shiny steel is manufactured each day.

321
00:23:53,361 --> 00:23:57,120
The process starts with the charging of scrap into the charging baskets.

322
00:23:57,720 --> 00:24:02,440
North American Stainless is probably one of the biggest recyclers of scrap in the United States.

323
00:24:02,800 --> 00:24:10,020
Every year we process about 780,000 tons of stainless scrap to manufacture a finished product.

324
00:24:10,021 --> 00:24:17,960
To boost the chrome level, workers turn to ferrochrome, an alloy of chrome and iron that

325
00:24:17,961 --> 00:24:23,580
contains between 45 and 65 percent chrome.

326
00:24:23,581 --> 00:24:28,860
It's added to the scrap to achieve at least 10.5 percent chromium content.

327
00:24:28,861 --> 00:24:33,020
Anything lower than that, and the resulting metal would not be stainless steel.

328
00:24:33,021 --> 00:24:35,960
Though it may not look the part.

329
00:24:35,961 --> 00:24:39,100
Adding just two tons of this granular chrome mixture

330
00:24:39,101 --> 00:24:43,960
to each 150-ton batch of scrap ultimately adds luster

331
00:24:43,961 --> 00:24:45,800
and corrosion resistance to the steel.

332
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,780
Once collected, an overhead crane dumps the raw materials

333
00:24:53,781 --> 00:24:55,020
into a massive furnace.

334
00:25:05,960 --> 00:25:08,740
We use an electric arc furnace.

335
00:25:08,840 --> 00:25:10,200
We have two electric arc furnaces.

336
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:16,120
The transformer size for one of those furnaces is probably 132 megawatts.

337
00:25:16,500 --> 00:25:21,500
A transformer of that size would light the downtown area of Louisville.

338
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:28,120
After reaching a temperature of 2200 degrees Fahrenheit in 45 minutes,

339
00:25:28,740 --> 00:25:31,800
now molten metal heads to a refining vessel.

340
00:25:31,801 --> 00:25:37,900
We'll be blowing argon and oxygen into the bottom of the vessel to remove the impurities

341
00:25:37,901 --> 00:25:40,480
in the steel, carbon and sulfur.

342
00:25:40,481 --> 00:25:45,840
We'll stop midpoint and take a test sample from the vessel and we'll be at that point

343
00:25:45,841 --> 00:25:47,560
checking the chrome content.

344
00:25:47,561 --> 00:25:52,560
If the chrome content is not as we want, we have an overhead alloy system where we add

345
00:25:52,561 --> 00:25:55,400
further chrome.

346
00:25:55,401 --> 00:26:00,880
While stainless steel may have as little as 10.5% chromium, this particular mixture

347
00:26:00,881 --> 00:26:06,520
destined for flatware production requires 18% chrome.

348
00:26:06,521 --> 00:26:09,640
The higher percentage of chromium is essential for the flatware

349
00:26:09,641 --> 00:26:13,040
to withstand a house full of corrosive agents.

350
00:26:13,041 --> 00:26:15,980
Many of the household items that we take for granted are caustic,

351
00:26:15,981 --> 00:26:17,480
and they eat into steel.

352
00:26:17,481 --> 00:26:20,120
In this experiment, we took common household items,

353
00:26:20,121 --> 00:26:23,160
such as vinegar, and we applied it to one sample,

354
00:26:23,161 --> 00:26:26,160
which was stainless steel, and the other, a low carbon steel.

355
00:26:26,161 --> 00:26:29,320
What we're doing is we're going to remove the vinegar from here.

356
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:31,740
But as we remove it, we can see that there's no rust.

357
00:26:31,741 --> 00:26:40,160
In this, the low carbon steel, as we remove the vinegar, there's a different story.

358
00:26:40,380 --> 00:26:44,060
Because of the lack of chrome, we see the corrosion process begin.

359
00:26:45,580 --> 00:26:49,120
Once the molten metal reaches the specified chrome concentration,

360
00:26:49,460 --> 00:26:54,620
it's molded and then cut by 4,000 degree Fahrenheit torch flames

361
00:26:54,621 --> 00:27:01,580
into 35-ton, 43-foot-long, semi-finished forms called slabs.

362
00:27:03,080 --> 00:27:06,000
They're brought to the hot mill furnace, which is behind me here.

363
00:27:06,520 --> 00:27:08,820
At that point in time, they are placed into the furnace,

364
00:27:09,220 --> 00:27:12,540
and they're in the furnace approximately two and a half hours

365
00:27:12,541 --> 00:27:18,140
to reheat those slabs to around 2,320 degrees Fahrenheit

366
00:27:18,141 --> 00:27:21,440
to reduce their strength so we're able to hot roll the product.

367
00:27:24,620 --> 00:27:35,940
The hot mill uses two rollers to compress the 43-foot long 8-inch slab to a 2,000-foot

368
00:27:35,941 --> 00:27:40,580
long coil just under a fifth of an inch thick.

369
00:27:40,581 --> 00:27:46,000
Next, the new stainless steel undergoes a treatment called annealing.

370
00:27:46,001 --> 00:27:54,460
The steel is heated and cooled to relieve internal stresses and make the metal less brittle.

371
00:27:54,461 --> 00:28:01,340
After that, an acid treatment called pickling removes any surface contaminants.

372
00:28:01,341 --> 00:28:08,120
Finally the stainless steel is cleaned, polished and cut to length before heading to fabricators

373
00:28:08,121 --> 00:28:12,020
across the country.

374
00:28:12,021 --> 00:28:17,080
Plantware made from stainless steel coils is a billion dollar industry.

375
00:28:17,081 --> 00:28:23,740
At Sherrill Manufacturing in Sherrill, New York, coils become more than 20,000 forks,

376
00:28:23,741 --> 00:28:26,040
spoons, and knives every day.

377
00:28:26,660 --> 00:28:29,260
Although Sherrill's company, Liberty Tabletop,

378
00:28:29,540 --> 00:28:33,040
is the only flatware manufacturer left in the United States,

379
00:28:33,740 --> 00:28:38,660
it's one of the most technologically sophisticated in the world.

380
00:28:39,940 --> 00:28:43,220
Any flatware you see that has USA on the back stamp,

381
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:44,520
it was made in this factory.

382
00:28:44,521 --> 00:28:46,340
We have a lot of automated equipment

383
00:28:46,341 --> 00:28:50,400
that allows us to use a lot less labor per piece.

384
00:28:50,780 --> 00:28:52,800
If this factory was running at full capacity,

385
00:28:52,801 --> 00:28:55,240
we could do about 400,000 pieces in a day.

386
00:28:56,880 --> 00:28:58,100
To make a fork,

387
00:28:58,580 --> 00:29:01,280
you typically use 18-8 stainless steel.

388
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:06,060
That means the steel contains 18% chrome and 8% nickel.

389
00:29:07,440 --> 00:29:09,860
The nickel increases the corrosion resistance.

390
00:29:10,360 --> 00:29:12,820
It also makes the part easier to manufacture.

391
00:29:13,240 --> 00:29:15,400
It's softer. It doesn't harden as fast.

392
00:29:15,760 --> 00:29:18,340
When you have 18% chrome and no nickel,

393
00:29:18,920 --> 00:29:20,400
the part becomes very brittle.

394
00:29:20,401 --> 00:29:28,340
The manufacturing process starts by feeding a 650-foot long stainless steel coil through

395
00:29:28,341 --> 00:29:32,740
a 150-ton blanking press.

396
00:29:32,741 --> 00:29:34,460
This is the piece that comes out.

397
00:29:34,461 --> 00:29:35,620
It's a fork blank.

398
00:29:35,621 --> 00:29:39,580
The thickness of the material is one hundred and five thousandths of an inch.

399
00:29:39,581 --> 00:29:42,160
From this coil, we can get about 16,000 forks.

400
00:29:42,161 --> 00:29:49,160
We'll go through about three of these coils a day, which is 7,500 pounds.

401
00:29:49,161 --> 00:29:54,240
After the press, the flat forks continue on to get their tines or prongs.

402
00:29:54,241 --> 00:29:59,720
To prevent the resulting prongs from bending, a so-called tine bar holds them together.

403
00:29:59,721 --> 00:30:06,560
Then we'll go into a stamping process where we put the pattern into the handle, and then

404
00:30:06,561 --> 00:30:09,200
the machine next to that will put the bowl on it.

405
00:30:09,201 --> 00:30:13,000
We strike the handle and raise the bowl in a coining press.

406
00:30:13,001 --> 00:30:16,300
It's very similar to making a coin, only a lot slower.

407
00:30:16,301 --> 00:30:21,300
These pieces run about 5,000 pieces an hour.

408
00:30:21,301 --> 00:30:24,300
Once shaped, machines remove the tine bar

409
00:30:24,301 --> 00:30:26,300
and delicately buff the prongs.

410
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:34,340
Then the forks pass through an eight-step buffing

411
00:30:34,341 --> 00:30:36,940
and polishing machine.

412
00:30:36,941 --> 00:30:39,860
After the pieces are buffed, they come to this machine here

413
00:30:39,861 --> 00:30:42,420
behind me, which is an ultrasonic wash.

414
00:30:42,421 --> 00:30:45,780
It uses detergent and ultrasonic waves to clean them.

415
00:30:46,640 --> 00:30:49,580
And then they're able to go into a bag nice and clean for the customer.

416
00:30:50,580 --> 00:30:54,420
So our homes are filled with chrome, sometimes by the bag full.

417
00:30:55,500 --> 00:30:59,840
But sometimes, if you really want to see the beauty of chrome in all its glory,

418
00:30:59,841 --> 00:31:01,760
there's only one thing to do.

419
00:31:02,020 --> 00:31:03,100
Just look up.

420
00:31:04,820 --> 00:31:06,240
The Chrysler Building.

421
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:09,940
Thanks to chrome, the nearly 100-year-old building

422
00:31:09,941 --> 00:31:12,980
is one of the most recognized skyscrapers in the world.

423
00:31:13,840 --> 00:31:16,180
The chrome gives it that brilliance, the shininess,

424
00:31:16,480 --> 00:31:18,420
and that is one of the most extraordinary things

425
00:31:18,421 --> 00:31:19,740
about the top of the Chrysler building.

426
00:31:19,880 --> 00:31:21,820
On a day like today, with the sun shining,

427
00:31:22,180 --> 00:31:25,720
it glints off of the metal, and it looks just stunning.

428
00:31:26,440 --> 00:31:29,120
In fact, the special stainless steel

429
00:31:29,121 --> 00:31:33,140
that glistens in the New York sun contains 18% chrome.

430
00:31:33,141 --> 00:31:41,040
And it was actually selected by Walter Chrysler himself for its performance characteristics

431
00:31:41,041 --> 00:31:44,400
and that it looked a lot like the chrome used on cars.

432
00:31:44,401 --> 00:31:50,420
He had it tested at his laboratories in Michigan and found that it was going to perform very

433
00:31:50,421 --> 00:31:52,640
well.

434
00:31:52,641 --> 00:31:57,640
The automobile tycoon chose the so-called Nyrosta metal due to the fact that it didn't

435
00:31:57,641 --> 00:32:02,460
rust and never had to be cleaned or polished to maintain its luster.

436
00:32:02,461 --> 00:32:10,420
Created in the 1920s by Krupp Metalwork of Germany, Nyrosta was the first stainless steel

437
00:32:10,421 --> 00:32:14,480
used in a major architectural project.

438
00:32:14,481 --> 00:32:18,460
The use of this chrome heavy metal along with the sleek and modern design of the dome and

439
00:32:18,461 --> 00:32:25,580
spire was very characteristic of the Art Deco design movement of the time.

440
00:32:25,581 --> 00:32:29,540
This was a period, you know, the whole idea of we're in the modern world.

441
00:32:29,541 --> 00:32:31,840
Let's have modern styles.

442
00:32:31,841 --> 00:32:35,100
Let's have forward-looking things.

443
00:32:35,101 --> 00:32:41,040
Art Deco takes its name from a design exposition held in Paris in 1925.

444
00:32:41,041 --> 00:32:48,900
American consumers found the geometric style of metallic materials elegant and functional.

445
00:32:48,901 --> 00:32:56,940
Soon designers were using chrome on everything, from furniture to toasters.

446
00:32:56,941 --> 00:33:01,440
And today, the Chrysler Building remains the most famous Art Deco skyscraper.

447
00:33:01,441 --> 00:33:09,440
Those receding circles rising up with the triangular windows and merging into that 185-foot

448
00:33:09,441 --> 00:33:12,780
spire rising into the sky, you can't miss it.

449
00:33:12,781 --> 00:33:17,820
Add to that all of the decorative features, the metal, it's got all of the geometric effects.

450
00:33:17,821 --> 00:33:19,060
There's just nothing quite like it.

451
00:33:19,061 --> 00:33:23,200
It is the Art Deco skyscraper.

452
00:33:23,201 --> 00:33:26,822
started in 1928 and took just 18 months.

453
00:33:26,823 --> 00:33:31,440
In a shop set up inside the building, metal workers

454
00:33:31,441 --> 00:33:33,427
measured and cut the nyrosta.

455
00:33:33,428 --> 00:33:37,400
Then roofers attached and joined the shiny metal together,

456
00:33:37,401 --> 00:33:42,615
piece by piece, to create the entire facade above the 61st floor.

457
00:33:42,616 --> 00:33:44,380
It was all custom hand

458
00:33:44,381 --> 00:33:45,840
fabricated work.

459
00:33:45,841 --> 00:33:52,320
Even the famous pineapples, radiator caps, and eagles were fashioned by

460
00:33:52,321 --> 00:33:59,320
by hand from Nyrosta, and then attached to iron frames protruding from the building.

461
00:34:01,520 --> 00:34:06,900
These are what we called the flying radiator caps, or wings of mercury, but it's basically

462
00:34:06,901 --> 00:34:11,560
a replica of the 1929 radiator cap from a Chrysler.

463
00:34:11,561 --> 00:34:18,180
Looking at this piece, it probably came as a hemisphere on that disk, a hemisphere on

464
00:34:18,181 --> 00:34:22,180
On this disc, this would have been a separate piece.

465
00:34:22,181 --> 00:34:27,160
Then that crowning element may have been the last part.

466
00:34:27,161 --> 00:34:32,100
The automobile iconography is throughout the building, but here, especially on the 31st

467
00:34:32,101 --> 00:34:33,700
floor, it's really apparent.

468
00:34:33,701 --> 00:34:39,880
You start with a radiator cap, much larger scale, and then down over on the wall here,

469
00:34:39,881 --> 00:34:45,420
you can see there's a replication of the wheels and the hubcaps from a Chrysler.

470
00:34:45,421 --> 00:34:48,580
So they're much larger than they are in the car,

471
00:34:48,581 --> 00:34:51,020
but they're integrated into the architecture

472
00:34:51,021 --> 00:34:54,320
in a very creative and frankly fun way.

473
00:34:54,321 --> 00:34:58,860
Stainless steel also cloaks the 185 foot vertex,

474
00:34:58,861 --> 00:35:01,280
but the sparkling needle piercing the sky

475
00:35:01,281 --> 00:35:03,880
wasn't always part of Chrysler's design.

476
00:35:03,881 --> 00:35:06,520
In fact, the structure was added solely

477
00:35:06,521 --> 00:35:08,940
to make the skyscraper taller.

478
00:35:08,941 --> 00:35:16,420
That enormous piece of steel that you see rising at the very top

479
00:35:16,421 --> 00:35:19,620
was brought to the building secretly in five pieces,

480
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:22,620
welded together in the dark of night,

481
00:35:22,720 --> 00:35:27,460
and then as dawn broke, it rose with a winch right up through the top,

482
00:35:27,620 --> 00:35:31,140
bringing the height of the building to 1,046 feet

483
00:35:31,141 --> 00:35:33,560
and claiming the title of world's tallest building,

484
00:35:34,020 --> 00:35:36,140
which it held for almost 18 months

485
00:35:36,141 --> 00:35:38,500
because then the Empire State Building opened,

486
00:35:38,501 --> 00:35:39,620
and that was the end of that.

487
00:35:41,080 --> 00:35:43,760
In all, 40 tons of Nyrostom metal

488
00:35:43,761 --> 00:35:46,100
containing more than 7 tons of chrome

489
00:35:46,101 --> 00:35:48,220
was used throughout the skyscraper.

490
00:35:48,740 --> 00:35:51,580
And the sparkling metal has certainly held up over time.

491
00:35:52,580 --> 00:35:56,780
Since the Chrysler building opened its doors on May 27, 1930,

492
00:35:57,420 --> 00:36:00,200
the chrome spire, dome, and adornments

493
00:36:00,201 --> 00:36:02,140
have only been cleaned twice.

494
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:06,400
Today, chrome is still making a statement

495
00:36:06,401 --> 00:36:07,640
in the world of architecture.

496
00:36:08,500 --> 00:36:14,100
Designers have once again turned to Nairostas Stainless Steel for the construction of the

497
00:36:14,101 --> 00:36:23,620
world's tallest building, the 2,716 and a half foot tall Burj Khalifa in Dubai, United Arab Emirates.

498
00:36:23,621 --> 00:36:31,460
The facade of the unparalleled skyscraper contains more than 400 tons of stainless steel.

499
00:36:31,461 --> 00:36:35,860
That's 10 times more than the Chrysler Building.

500
00:36:35,861 --> 00:36:41,860
But even so, New York's brilliant icon won't lose its fans.

501
00:36:41,861 --> 00:36:45,860
It's so high up in the air that the sun is always reflecting off of it.

502
00:36:45,861 --> 00:36:50,860
And no matter where you are in the city, you can see the Chrysler Building, and its gleam is always there.

503
00:36:54,860 --> 00:36:59,860
In Denver, Colorado, Sean Guerrero is hard at work on his latest masterpiece.

504
00:36:59,861 --> 00:37:05,820
When completed, it'll be a life-sized horse made entirely of recycled chrome bumpers.

505
00:37:06,980 --> 00:37:12,620
I'm not the only one who has built these sculptures from the medium.

506
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:14,520
This is just my way of doing it.

507
00:37:14,740 --> 00:37:19,360
But the chrome definitely gives it some kind of rustic elegance.

508
00:37:21,320 --> 00:37:24,000
Guerrero has created dozens of chrome sculptures,

509
00:37:24,460 --> 00:37:29,100
ranging from cowboys to giant roosters to dragons.

510
00:37:29,860 --> 00:37:33,700
Eagles and horses are his most popular pieces.

511
00:37:34,780 --> 00:37:39,380
This was my very first horse that I did probably 30 years ago.

512
00:37:39,620 --> 00:37:46,080
And now that I look at it, I remember at the time I had no idea how I was going to even build a horse.

513
00:37:46,580 --> 00:37:49,500
The work that I've done since then has really evolved.

514
00:37:50,040 --> 00:37:52,260
This piece here is kind of like a landmark.

515
00:37:52,261 --> 00:37:56,080
It's funny for me to see it up so close again after all this time.

516
00:37:56,081 --> 00:38:01,240
The owners of this location have moved on to other ventures,

517
00:38:01,420 --> 00:38:04,780
and today Guerrero's chrome stallion has a new home.

518
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:10,400
But for years, it was proudly displayed at this scrapyard where Guerrero got started.

519
00:38:13,460 --> 00:38:17,500
I just remember all the times that I used to come down here,

520
00:38:17,640 --> 00:38:18,900
and I was like a kid.

521
00:38:18,980 --> 00:38:21,360
I was just kind of mesmerized by all this chrome,

522
00:38:21,660 --> 00:38:22,780
especially in the summertime.

523
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:24,740
It's very bright, and you see it all.

524
00:38:24,741 --> 00:38:28,120
It's just shining. It's brilliant.

525
00:38:30,340 --> 00:38:34,300
The endless gleaming rose may look like a bumper graveyard to the layman,

526
00:38:34,940 --> 00:38:37,220
but to Guerrero, it's chrome heaven.

527
00:38:39,720 --> 00:38:42,680
When I'm looking around for a certain type of piece,

528
00:38:42,980 --> 00:38:44,600
usually it's something like this.

529
00:38:44,700 --> 00:38:48,160
I've got a big mass of metal right in here

530
00:38:48,161 --> 00:38:52,560
that I know when I bring it back to the shop I can actually cut up.

531
00:38:52,561 --> 00:38:56,940
like this section here might form part of the neck on a horse.

532
00:38:57,480 --> 00:39:01,940
Let's say, for example, I'm building some legs for an animal.

533
00:39:02,480 --> 00:39:06,480
Well, then I would look more for something like this here.

534
00:39:07,720 --> 00:39:09,720
I think this might be off of a Mustang.

535
00:39:11,100 --> 00:39:16,140
But these here, with these curves, are more natural,

536
00:39:16,141 --> 00:39:22,960
kind of akin to the way muscle structure on a horse might be.

537
00:39:24,640 --> 00:39:27,440
Sometimes Guerrero knows exactly what he's looking for.

538
00:39:28,020 --> 00:39:30,900
Other times, the chrome bumpers give him inspiration.

539
00:39:32,260 --> 00:39:38,900
These here, what gave me my first idea when I built a knight on a horse,

540
00:39:39,060 --> 00:39:42,340
and when I was looking at this particular shape here,

541
00:39:42,341 --> 00:39:46,220
It reminded me of the helmet on a knight.

542
00:39:47,040 --> 00:39:50,520
If you could imagine the back part here coming around

543
00:39:50,521 --> 00:39:56,280
and then some sort of faceplate in here with a head plume coming up,

544
00:39:56,580 --> 00:39:58,260
that's what I saw.

545
00:39:59,740 --> 00:40:05,120
This is one of the newer bumpers that I picked up over there at the yard,

546
00:40:05,400 --> 00:40:09,000
and I'm going to cut some pieces out of this

547
00:40:09,001 --> 00:40:12,940
for certain areas that I need on the horse that I'm working on inside.

548
00:40:13,800 --> 00:40:17,440
So normally I just kind of sketch something out with this soapstone here.

549
00:40:18,040 --> 00:40:19,840
Just to give me something to follow,

550
00:40:20,880 --> 00:40:23,800
this is the device that I use to cut.

551
00:40:24,220 --> 00:40:25,840
It's called a plasma machine,

552
00:40:26,700 --> 00:40:30,080
and what it basically does is it provides an electrical arc.

553
00:40:32,060 --> 00:40:36,160
The plasma cutting machine creates a clean and very precise cut,

554
00:40:36,161 --> 00:40:40,320
and it takes just a few minutes to chop up an entire bumper.

555
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:47,580
Then, Guerrero welds the pieces into place,

556
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:53,180
each one bringing the chrome statue one step closer to its finished form.

557
00:40:54,780 --> 00:40:59,720
They may start as junk, but the finished sculptures cost as much as $70,000.

558
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:02,760
The way I see it, when I was a little kid,

559
00:41:02,761 --> 00:41:07,360
I remember always liking things like the chrome fenders on the Schwinn Stingray.

560
00:41:07,560 --> 00:41:09,240
That, for me, had an appeal.

561
00:41:09,940 --> 00:41:11,460
And I think it's human nature.

562
00:41:11,580 --> 00:41:14,460
We enjoy those types of things, even to this day,

563
00:41:14,580 --> 00:41:17,000
whether it's a shiny, nice, fresh-painted house,

564
00:41:17,500 --> 00:41:20,860
a brand-new Mercedes-Benz, a diamond ring.

565
00:41:20,861 --> 00:41:23,980
We, as humans, are attracted to those objects.

566
00:41:24,300 --> 00:41:27,260
It's just something that's very deep-rooted within our psyche.

567
00:41:28,960 --> 00:41:31,240
Maybe that's what's behind chrome's appeal.

568
00:41:31,241 --> 00:41:37,000
whether it's on sculptures or buildings, trucks or motorcycles.

569
00:41:37,780 --> 00:41:42,800
It's the metal that raises our spirits and mirrors our desires.

